Q: How to preview and test the order received page? A: <p>By default, WooCommerce doesn't include any links to the order-received page in wp-admin, but you can add one to the &quot;Edit order&quot; page via the <code>$order-&gt;get_checkout_order_received_url()</code> hook.</p>
<pre><code>function add_order_received_page_link_to_order_editing_screen($order)
{
    echo '&lt;a href=&quot;' . $order-&gt;get_checkout_order_received_url() . '&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; class=&quot;button&quot; style=&quot;margin-top:10px&quot;&gt;' . __( 'View order received page', 'woocommerce' ) . '&lt;/a&gt;';
}
add_action('woocommerce_admin_order_data_after_order_details', 'add_order_received_page_link_to_order_editing_screen', 10, 1);
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.imgur.com/8hlRG1n.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.imgur.com/8hlRG1n.png" alt="Example of the View Order Received Page button" /></a></p>

Q: ?How to check if a Spring @Transactional method is actually running in a transaction? A: <p>You can use <code>TransactionSynchronizationManager</code> to check this at runtime:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class MyService {
    @Transactional
    public void doSomething() {
        boolean isActive = TransactionSynchronizationManager.isActualTransactionActive();
        System.out.println(&quot;Transaction active: &quot; + isActive); // true
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>@Transactional</code> is working correctly, <code>isActualTransactionActive()</code> returns true.</p>
<p><em><strong>Note:</strong></em> Make sure you're calling this method through a Spring proxy (i.e. from another bean), not from within the same class — otherwise <code>@Transactional</code> won't work at all.</p>
